<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <TargetPlatformMinVersion>10.0.17763.0</TargetPlatformMinVersion>
    <RootNamespace>Fastcull</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <Platforms>x86;x64;ARM64</Platforms>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x86;win-x64;win-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <PublishProfile>win-$(Platform).pubxml</PublishProfile>
    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="Assets\SplashScreen.scale-200.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\LockScreenLogo.scale-200.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\Square150x150Logo.scale-200.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\Square44x44Logo.scale-200.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\Square44x44Logo.targetsize-24_altform-unplated.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\StoreLogo.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\Wide310x150Logo.scale-200.png" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The sibling projects live under this project's directory, so the SDK's default globs
       would otherwise pull their sources into this assembly. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Remove="Fastcull.Tests\**;Fastcull.Core\**;Fastcull.Benchmarks\**" />
    <None Remove="Fastcull.Tests\**;Fastcull.Core\**;Fastcull.Benchmarks\**" />
    <Page Remove="Fastcull.Tests\**;Fastcull.Core\**;Fastcull.Benchmarks\**" />
    <PRIResource Remove="Fastcull.Tests\**;Fastcull.Core\**;Fastcull.Benchmarks\**" />
    <Content Remove="Fastcull.Tests\**;Fastcull.Core\**;Fastcull.Benchmarks\**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="Fastcull.Core\Fastcull.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Manifest Include="$(ApplicationManifest)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Defining the "Msix" ProjectCapability here allows the Single-project MSIX Packaging
    Tools extension to be activated for this project even if the Windows App SDK Nuget
    package has not yet been restored.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(DisableMsixProjectCapabilityAddedByProject)'!='true' and '$(EnableMsixTooling)'=='true'">
    <ProjectCapability Include="Msix" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.SDK.BuildTools" Version="10.0.28000.2526" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK" Version="2.4.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" Version="8.4.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Defining the "HasPackageAndPublishMenuAddedByProject" property here allows the Solution
    Explorer "Package and Publish" context menu entry to be enabled for this project even if
    the Windows App SDK Nuget package has not yet been restored.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(DisableHasPackageAndPublishMenuAddedByProject)'!='true' and '$(EnableMsixTooling)'=='true'">
    <HasPackageAndPublishMenu>true</HasPackageAndPublishMenu>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Publish Properties

    ============================================================================================
    DO NOT set PublishTrimmed back to True. A trimmed build of this app does not launch.
    ============================================================================================

    These two arrived from the WinUI project template, which enables both for any non-Debug
    configuration. That default is wrong for this app, and the failure it produces is a silent
    one: `dotnet publish` succeeds, the output folder looks complete, and the exe dies before it
    can draw a window.

    Verified 2026-08-24 with a clean Release publish (obj/ and bin/ deleted first, so this is
    trimming itself and not stale build artifacts):

        Fastcull.exe exits 0xC000027B  (STATUS_STOWED_EXCEPTION)
        faulting module Microsoft.UI.Xaml.dll, stowed HRESULT 0x80004003 (E_POINTER)

    The cause is that the trimmer removes types and members nothing references statically, while
    XAML resolves a great deal by name at runtime - converters, x:Bind generated code, and the
    CsWinRT marshalling shims. The build already tells us this is coming: the trimmer emits
    IL2104 for WinRT.Runtime, and the MVVM toolkit emits MVVMTK0045 for every [ObservableProperty]
    field, warning that the generated code is not AOT/trim safe in WinRT scenarios.

    PublishReadyToRun is off alongside it. R2R was never separately shown to break this app - it
    is pre-compilation rather than removal - but the only publish configuration that has actually
    been launched and exercised is the one with both disabled, so that is the one committed here.
    If you want the faster cold start back, validate it on its own and say so here.

    The practical consequence, and the reason this is pinned here rather than passed on the
    command line: the portable build in publish/FastCull-x64 is produced by a plain
    `dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 (self-contained) -p:Platform=x64
    -p:WindowsAppSDKSelfContained=true`. It has to be correct with no property overrides, because
    a broken portable build has already been shipped to that folder once by relying on someone
    remembering to pass them.

    Debug is unaffected either way - it never had these on.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PublishReadyToRun>False</PublishReadyToRun>
    <PublishTrimmed>False</PublishTrimmed>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>